  "slug": "expression-males-0059",
  "summary": "Cross-species atlases and sex-chromosome manipulations now resolve sex-biased expression and dosage compensation during prenatal development.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how sex chromosomes and sex-biased transcription shape gene expression in developing and adult tissues. In male mice, loss of the Xist locus, despite near-absent Xist expression, produces minor metabolic and memory effects, implicating a female-associated locus in male phenotypes. In embryonic chickens, comparison of sex chromosome and gonadal hormone manipulations indicates that chromosomal sex effects dominate hypothalamic gene expression. A cross-species single-cell atlas spanning mouse and human prenatal development maps sex-biased expression and X-to-autosome dosage compensation. A separate study traces sperm lipids to the morula stage, adding a distinct paternal contribution to early embryo metabolism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
